### Justification
The claim that sleeping under a mosquito bed net—whether treated or untreated with insecticide—is ineffective and harmful is directly contradicted by extensive authoritative evidence. A [Cochrane review of 22 trials across 17 countries](https://www.evidenceaid.org/wp-content/uploads/2013/08/Insecticide-treated_bed_nets_and_curtains_for_preventing.pdf) demonstrated that insecticide-treated bed nets (ITNs) reduce child mortality by 17–20%, cut malaria episodes by approximately 50%, and reduce severe malaria by 45%, while a [peer-reviewed study from The Gambia](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11706649/) showed that even untreated bed nets in good condition provide 51% protection against *Plasmodium falciparum* infection. On safety, the [U.S. CDC](https://www.cdc.gov/malaria/malaria_worldwide/reduction/itn.html) states that the insecticides used (pyrethroids and pyrroles) "pose very low health risks to humans and other mammals," and the [WHO](https://www.afro.who.int/news/treated-mosquito-nets-are-safe-and-effective) confirms that any reported side effects (headache, skin irritation, burning eyes, nausea) are "transient and mild," with no evidence linking ITN use to asthma or cancer. Major public health bodies—WHO, CDC, UNICEF, the President's Malaria Initiative, and the Roll Back Malaria Partnership—universally endorse ITNs as safe and effective, making the claim an outlier that contradicts the established scientific and public health consensus.